Tests the gamecore networking transport between separate processes.

Run with no arguments to run every scenario. For each one, a server process and two client processes are started on localhost and
the clients exchange reliable and unreliable messages with the server over a simulated link (packet loss, duplication, latency and
jitter). The exit code is zero if every scenario passed.

    net_test

The server and client can also be run by hand, for example to test between two machines:

    net_test server --host 0.0.0.0 --port 46960 --clients 1
    net_test client --host 192.168.1.10 --port 46960 --loss 20 --duplicate 5 --latency 40 --jitter 30

Options:
    --host ADDRESS      address to listen on (server) or connect to (client). Default 127.0.0.1
    --port PORT         default 46960
    --clients N         server only: exit after this many clients have finished. Default 1
    --messages N        client only: number of reliable (and unreliable) messages to send. Default 300
    --timeout SECONDS   give up after this long. Default 90
    --strict            client only: fail if any unreliable message was lost (only makes sense without the simulator)
    --expect-no-server  client only: pass if the connection attempt fails cleanly
    --shutdown-server   client only: send the "shutdown" event instead of test messages and pass if the server then reports
                        that it is shutting down. Also works against dedicated_server
    --loss PERCENT      link simulator: chance of dropping a packet
    --duplicate PERCENT link simulator: chance of duplicating a packet
    --latency MS        link simulator: delay added to every packet
    --jitter MS         link simulator: random variation of the delay

The link simulator affects every packet the process sends and receives.
